    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener><dateline>143 East Adams avenue, <lb/><rs ref="#tillich_place_id__1045"
                            type="place">Kirkwood</rs>, 22, <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__798"
                            type="place">Missouri</rs>, <lb/><date when="1946-08-20">August 20,
                            1946</date>
                    </dateline>
                    <salute>My dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Dr.
                            Tillich</rs>:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>Some of your former students have written to me to inform me that today is your
                    birthday. While I have never had the privilege of studying under you directly, I
                    am more than happy to be able to add my felicitations to theirs on this
                    occasion.</p>
                <p>Indeed, I take it as a very fine compliment that the nature of my writings is
                    such that I am classified with those who have sat at your feet. As a matter of
                    fact, your influence upon my thinking and writing has been very pronounced – –
                    both through your books and articles, and through <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__1390" type="person">Niebuhr</rs> and <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__3918" type="person">Clifford Stanley</rs>.</p>
                <p>When I was a student at the General Theological Seminary some ten or more years
                    ago, I had the assignment to read a paper on the nature of the Church at an
                    inter-seminary gathering at Union, with <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1464"
                        type="person">Pauck</rs> presiding and with you as discussion leader. That
                    was my first direct contact with you. It was rather an awesome occasion for a
                    young seminarian, no matter how brash he might be.</p>
                <p>With these words of explanation, may I once more join with those who have studied
                    directly under you, and express my wishes for many very happy returns of this
                    day. I look forward to learning much more from your continued leadership in the
                    field of relevant Christianity.</p>
                <closer><salute>Very sincerely</salute>
                    <signed><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3883" type="person">Charles D.
                        Kean</rs></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
